  "updated": "2026-08-11T19:31:28.823856+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern late this morning is defined by\nan upper-level ridge of high pressure centered just east of the\nFour Corners and an inverted trough oriented SW-NE across Chihuahua\nMexico. The moisture profile is defined by the GOES precipitable\nwater imagery, indicating some drier air hanging out along the \nAZ/NM border (PWATs 1.00-1.10 inches), with deeper moisture \nmoving east into Tucson (PWATs 1.25 inches) and very deep\nmoisture PWATs between 1.50-2.00 inches across the western \ndeserts. \n\nI have been around here long enough to know that the bigger and\nmore active thunderstorm days for Southeast Arizona usually \nalign with big movements of the upper high. Today is no different.\nThe upper-high center near the Four Corners will move east over\nthe next 24 to 36 hours, eventually making its way to central \nOklahoma by 13/00Z Wednesday afternoon. As this happens, not \nonly will we experience general deformation aloft assisting \nlift across the region, but that inverted trough in Chihuahua \nMexico will rotate up into the far SE corner of AZ by this \nafternoon providing a better lifting mechanism. The best lift \ntoday will initially occur across SRN Cochise early this \nafternoon, then expand toward the NW into north-central Pima and\nSE Pinal counties by 12/06Z this evening. \n\nThe drier airmass residing along the AZ/NM border today will \nincrease the potential for strong organized thunderstorm outflows\nwith the initial development. This is supported by the 11/12Z \nHREF which suggests a 10 percent probability for wind gusts 50+ \nkts in SE Cochise county this afternoon. As the storms progress \nto the northwest, they move into that deeper moist airmass which\nshifts the potential from wind to heavy rain. The 11/12Z HREF \n3-hr probability-matched mean QPF indicates a 10 percent \nprobability for rainfall in excess of 3 inches across southern \nCochise and Santa Cruz counties between 2-5 pm MST, expanding \nacross the eastern half of Pima county and SE Pinal counties \nbetween 5-8 pm MST. Given this, and the deep moisture to the \nwest that would feed into the storms in the low-to-mid levels, \nwe decided to expand the coverage of the Flash Flood Watch to \ninclude central Pima county (including the Tohono O'odham \nNation) as well as SE Pinal county. My concern is that as the \nupper-high center moves east, the NW progress of the convection \nwill stall out across the central portions of the CWA late this \nevening and stick around through the night. Granted, the \nconvective complex will continue to weaken, but even moderate \nrainfall over the same spot will keep the concern for flash \nflooding into the early morning hours.\n\nOtherwise, Wednesday and Thursday still look quite wet as the\nflow becomes southerly and any wiggle/disturbance in the flow\nwill easily result in convection across our neck of the woods. \nRe-iterating the previous forecast discussion...expect a downturn\nin daily thunderstorm activity Friday into the weekend as drier\nair pushes across the area under southwest flow aloft. Isolated\nthunderstorms at best, and mostly east of Tucson. Temperatures \nwill be near normal today, then be about 3 to 6 degrees below \nnormal Wednesday and Thursday, with warming thereafter to above \nnormal values for this weekend.",
